    "markdown": "# Static Arrays: Stack Boundaries & Fixed Memory Blocks\n\n![Static Arrays: Stack Boundaries & Fixed Memory Blocks](/images/dsa/static-arrays-dark.jpg#dark)\n\nIn modern programming runtimes, variables are often treated as dynamic allocations that grow and shrink at will. However, in systems programming and operating system kernels, allocating memory dynamically is a high-overhead operation. \n\nThis chapter explores **Static Arrays**—fixed-size, contiguous memory sequences allocated at compile-time directly on the stack or global data segment. We focus on physical cache layouts, offset address compilation, and compile-time boundaries.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Compile-Time Allocations: The Stack & Global Segment\n\nA static array is declared with a size that is fixed at compile-time:\n\n```cpp\nint buffer[5]; // Fixed size of 5 integers\n```\n\nWhen this line is compiled, the compiler calculates the exact byte requirements of the array:\n\n$$\\text{Bytes} = \\text{ElementSize} \\times \\text{Length} = 4 \\text{ bytes} \\times 5 = 20 \\text{ bytes}$$\n\nBecause the size is known at compile-time, the compiler does not query the heap allocator. Instead:\n- If the array is local to a function, the compiler simply shifts the **Stack Pointer** register (`RSP`) down by 20 bytes when entering the function frame.\n- If the array is global, it is allocated in the program's binary **BSS** or **Data** segment, loaded directly into RAM when the process starts.\n\n### Stack Frame Buffer Boundaries\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 600 200\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <rect width=\"600\" height=\"200\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#000\" stroke=\"#27272a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text x=\"30\" y=\"35\" fill=\"#3b82f6\" font-size=\"13\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-weight=\"bold\">Compile-Time Stack Frame Array Allocation</text>\n\n  <g transform=\"translate(45, 65)\">\n    {/* Stack Frame bounds */}\n    <rect x=\"0\" y=\"10\" width=\"220\" height=\"80\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#71717a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"110\" y=\"5\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">Function Stack Frame</text>\n    \n    {/* Static Array */}\n    <rect x=\"20\" y=\"30\" width=\"180\" height=\"40\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#1e1b4b\" stroke=\"#3b82f6\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <text x=\"110\" y=\"54\" fill=\"#3b82f6\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">buffer[5] (20 Bytes Contiguous)</text>\n\n    {/* RSP Pointer */}\n    <path d=\"M 280,50 L 235,50\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#4ade80\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-stack-static)\"/>\n    <text x=\"290\" y=\"53\" fill=\"#4ade80\" font-size=\"9\" font-family=\"monospace\">RSP Offset adjustment: -20B</text>\n  </g>\n\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"arrow-stack-static\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"5\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"4\" markerHeight=\"4\" orient=\"auto\">\n      <path d=\"M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\" fill=\"#4ade80\" />\n    </marker>\n  </defs>\n</svg>\n\nThere is absolute zero runtime allocator lookup overhead, making static arrays the fastest sequential structure possible in hardware.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Hard Limits: Preventing Buffer Overflow Vulnerabilities\n\nBecause static arrays sit directly in stack frames alongside local variables and function return addresses, they have no built-in safety boundaries. If code writes data past the end of the array, it overrides adjacent memory—a vulnerability known as a **Stack Buffer Overflow**.\n\n```c\nvoid vulnerable_function() {\n    char buffer[8];\n    strcpy(buffer, \"excessive_input_string\"); // Overrides buffer boundaries\n}\n```\n\n### Buffer Overflow Attack Mechanics\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 600 200\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <rect width=\"600\" height=\"200\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#000\" stroke=\"#27272a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text x=\"30\" y=\"35\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-size=\"13\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-weight=\"bold\">Stack Buffer Overflow Attack Pattern</text>\n\n  <g transform=\"translate(45, 65)\">\n    {/* Buffer */}\n    <rect x=\"0\" y=\"20\" width=\"120\" height=\"40\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#3b82f6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"60\" y=\"44\" fill=\"#3b82f6\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">buffer[8]</text>\n\n    {/* Saved Frame Pointer */}\n    <rect x=\"125\" y=\"20\" width=\"120\" height=\"40\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#a1a1aa\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    <text x=\"185\" y=\"44\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">Saved RBP Frame</text>\n\n    {/* Return Address */}\n    <rect x=\"250\" y=\"20\" width=\"140\" height=\"40\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"320\" y=\"44\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">Return Address Pointer</text>\n\n    {/* Override Flow */}\n    <path d=\"M -10,8 L 380,8\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 1\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-overflow-dir)\"/>\n    <text x=\"180\" y=\"2\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-size=\"8\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">Input write overrides bounds and updates Return Address</text>\n  </g>\n\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"arrow-overflow-dir\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"5\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"4\" markerHeight=\"4\" orient=\"auto\">\n      <path d=\"M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\" fill=\"#ef4444\" />\n    </marker>\n  </defs>\n</svg>\n\nIf a hacker sends input that overflows `buffer`, the excess bytes overwrite the **Return Address**. When the function returns, the CPU jumps to the hijacked address (e.g. executing malicious payload code). \n\n---\n\n## 3. How Real-World Systems Leverage Static Arrays\n\nSystems engineering targets static arrays when memory footprints must remain bounded and deterministic:\n\n### Embedded Systems & Avionics\nIn aerospace flight controllers (e.g., NASA's Mars Rover code), dynamic heap allocation (`malloc`/`free`) is forbidden. If the heap manager fragments memory, the application could fail to allocate memory during a critical operation. Instead, these systems pre-allocate all buffers as global static arrays.\n\n### Linux Network Interfaces: Ring Queues\nOperating system driver cards buffering incoming network packets map packet queues as fixed-length circular rings containing pre-allocated static byte structures. This guarantees packet processing at line speed with no allocator overhead.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Practice Checklist\n\n### Beginner Problems\n* [Two Sum (LeetCode 1)](https://leetcode.com/problems/two-sum/): Map index lookups.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Teaches you to coordinate linear elements.\n* [Remove Duplicates from Sorted Array (LeetCode 26)](https://leetcode.com/problems/remove-duplicates-from-sorted-array/): Edit static buffer in-place.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Build intuition on moving write boundaries within fixed limits.\n\n### Intermediate Problems\n* [Rotate Array (LeetCode 189)](https://leetcode.com/problems/rotate-array/): Cyclical index translations.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Practices shifting elements across modular boundaries without allocating extra arrays.\n* [Product of Array Except Self (LeetCode 238)](https://leetcode.com/problems/product-of-array-except-self/): Prefix/suffix arrays.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Demonstrates dynamic aggregation tables using simple fixed buffers.\n",
