    "markdown": "# Dynamic Vectors: Geometric Growth & Capacity Scaling\n\n![Dynamic Vectors: Geometric Growth & Capacity Scaling](/images/dsa/dynamic-vectors-dark.jpg#dark)\n\nWhile static arrays provide maximum performance, they require their sizes to be known at compile-time. If you are building a system where data arrives dynamically, you need a contiguous buffer that grows elastically.\n\nThis chapter explores **Dynamic Vectors** (known as `std::vector` in C++ or `ArrayList` in Java). We focus on the mathematics of geometric resizing, amortized complexity proofs, and pointer invalidation traps.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Elastic Allocations: Size vs. Capacity\n\nA dynamic vector manages a contiguous memory buffer on the heap. It exposes two size properties:\n1. **Size**: The number of actual elements currently stored.\n2. **Capacity**: The size of the underlying memory block allocated on the heap.\n\n```cpp\nstd::vector<int> vec; // Size: 0, Capacity: 0\nvec.push_back(10);    // Size: 1, Capacity: 1 (or default minimum)\n```\n\nWhen you push a new element into a vector:\n- If $\\text{Size} < \\text{Capacity}$, the value is written directly to the next index ($O(1)$).\n- If $\\text{Size} == \\text{Capacity}$, the vector must resize. It queries the allocator for a new, larger memory block, copies existing elements to the new block, deletes the old block, and writes the new element ($O(N)$).\n\n### Size vs. Capacity Growth\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 600 200\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <rect width=\"600\" height=\"200\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#000\" stroke=\"#27272a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text x=\"30\" y=\"35\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-size=\"13\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-weight=\"bold\">Vector Capacity Resizing Bounds</text>\n\n  <g transform=\"translate(45, 65)\">\n    {/* Vector Buffer */}\n    <rect x=\"0\" y=\"20\" width=\"160\" height=\"35\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#3b82f6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <rect x=\"5\" y=\"25\" width=\"30\" height=\"25\" fill=\"#1e293b\" stroke=\"#3b82f6\"/>\n    <text x=\"20\" y=\"41\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">10</text>\n    <rect x=\"40\" y=\"25\" width=\"30\" height=\"25\" fill=\"#1e293b\" stroke=\"#3b82f6\"/>\n    <text x=\"55\" y=\"41\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">20</text>\n    \n    <text x=\"80\" y=\"10\" fill=\"#71717a\" font-size=\"8\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">Size: 2 / Capacity: 4</text>\n\n    {/* Dynamic Reallocation */}\n    <path d=\"M 175,37 L 225,37\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-resize-vec)\"/>\n    <text x=\"200\" y=\"28\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-size=\"7\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">Resizing</text>\n\n    {/* New Buffer */}\n    <g transform=\"translate(240, 0)\">\n      <rect x=\"0\" y=\"20\" width=\"240\" height=\"35\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n      <rect x=\"5\" y=\"25\" width=\"30\" height=\"25\" fill=\"#1e293b\" stroke=\"#10b981\"/>\n      <text x=\"20\" y=\"41\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">10</text>\n      <rect x=\"40\" y=\"25\" width=\"30\" height=\"25\" fill=\"#1e293b\" stroke=\"#10b981\"/>\n      <text x=\"55\" y=\"41\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">20</text>\n      <rect x=\"75\" y=\"25\" width=\"30\" height=\"25\" fill=\"#1e293b\" stroke=\"#10b981\"/>\n      <text x=\"90\" y=\"41\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">30</text>\n      \n      <text x=\"120\" y=\"10\" fill=\"#71717a\" font-size=\"8\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">Size: 3 / Capacity: 8 (Doubled)</text>\n    </g>\n  </g>\n\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"arrow-resize-vec\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"5\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"4\" markerHeight=\"4\" orient=\"auto\">\n      <path d=\"M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\" fill=\"#ef4444\" />\n    </marker>\n  </defs>\n</svg>\n\n---\n\n## 2. Resizing Math: Amortized Constant Time Proof\n\nIf resizing copies elements ($O(N)$), why are vector insertions described as $O(1)$ operations? We prove this using **Amortized Analysis**.\n\nIf a vector expands its capacity **geometrically** (multiplying the capacity by a constant factor $F$, usually $2$ or $1.5$) whenever it is full:\n- Suppose we insert $N$ elements into an empty vector.\n- Resizing occurrences will happen at sizes: $1, 2, 4, 8, \\dots, N$.\n- The total elements copied during all resizes is:\n\n$$\\sum = 1 + 2 + 4 + 8 + \\dots + \\frac{N}{2} < N$$\n\n- The total operations for $N$ pushes is: $N$ writes + $N$ copies = $2N$ steps.\n- The average (amortized) cost per push is:\n\n$$\\text{Amortized Cost} = \\frac{2N}{N} = 2 = O(1)$$\n\nIf we had instead resized by a constant increment (e.g. adding 10 slots each time), the sum of copies would grow quadratically ($O(N^2)$), yielding an amortized cost of $O(N)$ per push. Hence, geometric growth is required for $O(1)$ amortized insertion performance.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Practice Checklist\n\n### Beginner Problems\n* [Merge Sorted Array (LeetCode 88)](https://leetcode.com/problems/merge-sorted-array/): In-place vectors.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Build intuition on merging indices within dynamic buffers.\n* [Move Zeroes (LeetCode 283)](https://leetcode.com/problems/move-zeroes/): Shift elements.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Teaches you to write dynamic pointer shifts.\n\n### Intermediate Problems\n* [3Sum (LeetCode 15)](https://leetcode.com/problems/3sum/): Two pointers in sorting.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Combines sorting vectors with matching constraints.\n* [Subarray Sum Equals K (LeetCode 560)](https://leetcode.com/problems/subarray-sum-equals-k/): Prefix sums in vectors.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Integrates array lookups with hash tracking.\n",
