    "markdown": "# Singly Linked Lists: Unidirectional Reference Chains\n\n![Singly Linked Lists: Unidirectional Reference Chains](/images/dsa/singly-linked-lists-dark.jpg#dark)\n\nA singly linked list is the simplest reference-based list structure. It connects elements sequentially using a single pointer per node, forming a unidirectional chain.\n\nThis chapter explores **Singly Linked Lists**. We focus on the mechanics of single-link manipulations, dummy sentinel node optimizations, and edge cases.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Node Topology: Unidirectional Pointers\n\nA singly linked list node contains a value payload and a single pointer (`next`) holding the physical address of the next node. Because pointers are unidirectional, you can only traverse the list forward from the head.\n\n```cpp\nstruct ListNode {\n    int val;\n    ListNode* next;\n    ListNode(int x) : val(x), next(nullptr) {}\n};\n```\n\n### Singly Linked Node Structure\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 600 180\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <rect width=\"600\" height=\"180\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#000\" stroke=\"#27272a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text x=\"30\" y=\"35\" fill=\"#f472b6\" font-size=\"13\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-weight=\"bold\">Singly Linked Node Linkage</text>\n\n  <g transform=\"translate(45, 60)\">\n    {/* Node 1 */}\n    <g>\n      <rect x=\"0\" y=\"10\" width=\"100\" height=\"40\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#f472b6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n      <line x1=\"60\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#f472b6\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n      <text x=\"30\" y=\"34\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">Val: 10</text>\n      <text x=\"80\" y=\"34\" fill=\"#f472b6\" font-size=\"8\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">0x50A0</text>\n    </g>\n\n    {/* Forward Link */}\n    <path d=\"M 90,30 L 165,30\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#f472b6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-singly)\"/>\n\n    {/* Node 2 */}\n    <g transform=\"translate(170, 0)\">\n      <rect x=\"0\" y=\"10\" width=\"100\" height=\"40\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#f472b6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n      <line x1=\"60\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#f472b6\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n      <text x=\"30\" y=\"34\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">Val: 20</text>\n      <text x=\"80\" y=\"34\" fill=\"#71717a\" font-size=\"8\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">null</text>\n    </g>\n  </g>\n\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"arrow-singly\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"5\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"4\" markerHeight=\"4\" orient=\"auto\">\n      <path d=\"M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\" fill=\"#f472b6\" />\n    </marker>\n  </defs>\n</svg>\n\nBecause there is no back-pointer, deleting a node at index $i$ requires you to walk from the head to find the predecessor node at index $i-1$, costing $O(i)$ traversal time.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Dynamic Insertions & Deletions\n\nInserting a new node into a singly linked list requires adjusting two pointers:\n1. Set the new node's `next` pointer to point to the successor node.\n2. Set the predecessor node's `next` pointer to point to the new node.\n\n### Singly Linked Node Insertion\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 600 200\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <rect width=\"600\" height=\"200\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#000\" stroke=\"#27272a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text x=\"30\" y=\"35\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-size=\"13\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-weight=\"bold\">Singly Linked List Insertion</text>\n\n  <g transform=\"translate(45, 60)\">\n    {/* Predecessor */}\n    <g>\n      <rect x=\"0\" y=\"10\" width=\"80\" height=\"40\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#f472b6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n      <text x=\"40\" y=\"34\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">Prev Node</text>\n    </g>\n\n    {/* New Node */}\n    <g transform=\"translate(120, 60)\">\n      <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"80\" height=\"40\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n      <text x=\"40\" y=\"24\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">New Node</text>\n    </g>\n\n    {/* Successor */}\n    <g transform=\"translate(240, 10)\">\n      <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"80\" height=\"40\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#f472b6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n      <text x=\"40\" y=\"24\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">Next Node</text>\n    </g>\n\n    {/* Old Link */}\n    <path d=\"M 80,30 L 235,30\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#71717a\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 1\"/>\n\n    {/* New rewiring links */}\n    <path d=\"M 80,30 Q 100,60 115,70\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-insert-singly)\"/>\n    <path d=\"M 200,80 Q 220,50 235,40\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-insert-singly)\"/>\n  </g>\n\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"arrow-insert-singly\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"5\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"4\" markerHeight=\"4\" orient=\"auto\">\n      <path d=\"M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\" fill=\"#10b981\" />\n    </marker>\n  </defs>\n</svg>\n\n---\n\n## 3. Practice Checklist\n\n### Beginner Problems\n* [Reverse Linked List (LeetCode 206)](https://leetcode.com/problems/reverse-linked-list/): Unidirectional loop.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Build core pointer manipulation skills.\n* [Merge Two Sorted Lists (LeetCode 21)](https://leetcode.com/problems/merge-two-sorted-lists/): Node comparisons.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Practices using pointer rewires to merge sorted chains.\n\n### Intermediate Problems\n* [Remove Nth Node From End of List (LeetCode 19)](https://leetcode.com/problems/remove-nth-node-from-end-of-list/): Two pointers.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Teaches you to use pointer offsets to find nodes from the end in a single pass.\n* [Add Two Numbers (LeetCode 2)](https://leetcode.com/problems/add-two-numbers/): Math addition chains.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Practices list building with carry operations.\n",
