  "slug": "double-ended-deques",
    "markdown": "# Double-Ended Deques: Bidirectional Buffers & Sliding Windows\n\n![Double-Ended Deques: Bidirectional Buffers & Sliding Windows](/images/dsa/double-ended-deques-dark.jpg#dark)\n\nStandard queues restrict enqueues to the back and dequeues to the front. If you need to insert or delete elements at both boundaries in constant time, you use a **Double-Ended Queue** (commonly called a **Deque**).\n\nThis chapter explores **Double-Ended Deques**. We focus on circular bidirectional offset arithmetic, sliding window optimizations, and queue bounds.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Bidirectional Pointers: Index wrapping on both ends\n\nA deque supports four operations, all running in $O(1)$ constant time:\n1. `push_front`: Insert an element at the front.\n2. `push_back`: Insert an element at the back.\n3. `pop_front`: Remove from the front.\n4. `pop_back`: Remove from the back.\n\nImplementing a deque using a circular array requires wrapping index calculations in both directions:\n- Moving the front pointer backward during `push_front`:\n\n$$\\text{front} = (\\text{front} - 1 + \\text{capacity}) \\bmod \\text{capacity}$$\n\n- Moving the rear pointer forward during `push_back`:\n\n$$\\text{rear} = (\\text{rear} + 1) \\bmod \\text{capacity}$$\n\n### Deque Bidirectional Operations\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 600 200\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <rect width=\"600\" height=\"200\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#000\" stroke=\"#27272a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text x=\"30\" y=\"35\" fill=\"#3b82f6\" font-size=\"13\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-weight=\"bold\">Deque Bidirectional Actions</text>\n\n  <g transform=\"translate(45, 60)\">\n    {/* Deque Box */}\n    <rect x=\"100\" y=\"20\" width=\"200\" height=\"35\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#3b82f6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"200\" y=\"42\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">Buffer Contents</text>\n\n    {/* Front operations */}\n    <path d=\"M 90,30 L 45,30\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-deque-dir)\"/>\n    <path d=\"M 45,45 L 90,45\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-deque-dir-r)\"/>\n    <text x=\"65\" y=\"20\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-size=\"7\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">Pop Front</text>\n    <text x=\"65\" y=\"58\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-size=\"7\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">Push Front</text>\n\n    {/* Back operations */}\n    <path d=\"M 310,30 L 355,30\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-deque-dir-r)\"/>\n    <path d=\"M 355,45 L 310,45\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-deque-dir)\"/>\n    <text x=\"335\" y=\"20\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-size=\"7\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">Push Back</text>\n    <text x=\"335\" y=\"58\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-size=\"7\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">Pop Back</text>\n  </g>\n\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"arrow-deque-dir\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"5\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"4\" markerHeight=\"4\" orient=\"auto\">\n      <path d=\"M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\" fill=\"#ef4444\" />\n    </marker>\n    <marker id=\"arrow-deque-dir-r\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"5\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"4\" markerHeight=\"4\" orient=\"auto\">\n      <path d=\"M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\" fill=\"#10b981\" />\n    </marker>\n  </defs>\n</svg>\n\n---\n\n## 2. Sliding Window Optimization: Monotonic Deques\n\nDeques are crucial for optimization in **Sliding Window** problems.\n\nConsider finding the maximum value in every sliding window of size $K$ in an array. A naive scan costs $O(N \\times K)$ time. \n\nBy using a **Monotonic Deque**, we can solve this in $O(N)$ linear time:\n1. Iterate through elements.\n2. Maintain a deque of indices, keeping values in decreasing order (`push_back` / `pop_back`).\n3. If the element at the front of the deque lies outside the window, `pop_front` it.\n4. The maximum value for the current window is always at `deque.front()`.\n\n### Sliding Window Deque Invariant\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 600 200\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <rect width=\"600\" height=\"200\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#000\" stroke=\"#27272a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text x=\"30\" y=\"35\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-size=\"13\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-weight=\"bold\">Sliding Window Monotonic Deque Invariant</text>\n\n  <g transform=\"translate(45, 60)\">\n    {/* Array values */}\n    <rect x=\"0\" y=\"20\" width=\"40\" height=\"30\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#71717a\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    <text x=\"20\" y=\"38\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">1</text>\n    \n    <rect x=\"45\" y=\"20\" width=\"40\" height=\"30\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#1e1b4b\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <text x=\"65\" y=\"38\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">3</text>\n    \n    <rect x=\"90\" y=\"20\" width=\"40\" height=\"30\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#1e1b4b\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <text x=\"110\" y=\"38\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">-1</text>\n\n    <text x=\"80\" y=\"10\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-size=\"8\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">Active Window (Size 2)</text>\n\n    {/* Deque contents */}\n    <g transform=\"translate(180, 0)\">\n      <rect x=\"0\" y=\"20\" width=\"100\" height=\"30\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n      <rect x=\"5\" y=\"25\" width=\"40\" height=\"20\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#10b981\"/>\n      <text x=\"25\" y=\"38\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">Idx 1 (val 3)</text>\n      \n      <text x=\"110\" y=\"38\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-size=\"8\" font-family=\"monospace\">◄ front() holds Max</text>\n    </g>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n\n---\n\n## 3. Practice Checklist\n\n### Beginner Problems\n* [Design Circular Deque (LeetCode 641)](https://leetcode.com/problems/design-circular-deque/): Build double circular array.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Master bidirectional index arithmetic.\n* [Linked List Cycle II (LeetCode 142)](https://leetcode.com/problems/linked-list-cycle-ii/): Loop checks.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Build loop detection checks.\n\n### Intermediate Problems\n* [Sliding Window Maximum (LeetCode 239)](https://leetcode.com/problems/sliding-window-maximum/): Monotonic deque bounds.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Applies deques to slide boundaries and track max values in linear time.\n* [Longest Continuous Subarray With Absolute Diff Less Than or Equal to Limit (LeetCode 1438)](https://leetcode.com/problems/longest-continuous-subarray-with-absolute-diff-less-than-or-equal-to-limit/): Dual deques.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Combines a min-deque and a max-deque to track rolling ranges.\n",
