  "slug": "min-heaps",
    "markdown": "# Min Heaps: Priority Extraction & Array-Backed Trees\n\n![Min Heaps: Priority Extraction & Array-Backed Trees](/images/dsa/min-heaps-dark.jpg#dark)\n\nIn scheduling systems, processing tasks in chronological or sorted order using simple lists costs $O(N)$ lookup scans. To select the minimum element in logarithmic time, we use a **Min Heap**.\n\nThis chapter explores **Min Heaps**. We focus on parent-child array indexing layouts, bubble-up/down sorting passes, and priority updates.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Array representation: Indexing Coordinates\n\nA min heap is a complete binary tree where the key of any node is less than or equal to the keys of its children. Because the tree is complete (filled level by level from left to right), it can be stored in a flat array with no pointer overhead:\n\n- The root element is stored at index `0`.\n- For any node at index `i`:\n  - **Left Child**: `2 * i + 1`\n  - **Right Child**: `2 * i + 2`\n  - **Parent**: `(i - 1) / 2` (using floor integer division)\n\n### Array-Backed Complete Binary Tree\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 600 200\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <rect width=\"600\" height=\"200\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#000\" stroke=\"#27272a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text x=\"30\" y=\"35\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-size=\"13\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-weight=\"bold\">Min Heap Array Indexing Mapping</text>\n\n  <g transform=\"translate(45, 60)\">\n    {/* Binary tree nodes */}\n    <circle cx=\"100\" cy=\"20\" r=\"14\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <text x=\"100\" y=\"23\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">10 (Idx 0)</text>\n\n    <path d=\"M 90,30 L 60,60\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"50\" cy=\"70\" r=\"14\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#3b82f6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"50\" y=\"73\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">15 (Idx 1)</text>\n\n    <path d=\"M 110,30 L 140,60\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"150\" cy=\"70\" r=\"14\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#3b82f6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"150\" y=\"73\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">20 (Idx 2)</text>\n\n    {/* Array layout */}\n    <g transform=\"translate(240, 40)\">\n      <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"180\" height=\"30\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#71717a\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n      <rect x=\"5\" y=\"5\" width=\"50\" height=\"20\" fill=\"#1e293b\" stroke=\"#10b981\"/>\n      <text x=\"30\" y=\"18\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">10</text>\n      <rect x=\"60\" y=\"5\" width=\"50\" height=\"20\" fill=\"#1e293b\" stroke=\"#3b82f6\"/>\n      <text x=\"85\" y=\"18\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">15</text>\n      <rect x=\"115\" y=\"5\" width=\"55\" height=\"20\" fill=\"#1e293b\" stroke=\"#3b82f6\"/>\n      <text x=\"142\" y=\"18\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">20</text>\n    </g>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n\n---\n\n## 2. Heapify Down Swaps\n\nWhen you remove the root element (extract-min):\n1. Replace the root with the last element in the array.\n2. Sift this element down (**Heapify Down**) by comparing it with its children.\n3. Swap it with the smaller child to maintain the min-heap property.\n4. Repeat until the element is smaller than or equal to both children.\n\n### Heapify Down Step\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 600 200\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <rect width=\"600\" height=\"200\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#000\" stroke=\"#27272a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text x=\"30\" y=\"35\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-size=\"13\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-weight=\"bold\">Heapify Down (Sifting Element)</text>\n\n  <g transform=\"translate(45, 60)\">\n    {/* Unbalanced Tree */}\n    <circle cx=\"100\" cy=\"20\" r=\"14\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <text x=\"100\" y=\"23\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">40</text>\n\n    <path d=\"M 90,30 L 60,60\" stroke=\"#71717a\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"50\" cy=\"70\" r=\"14\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"50\" y=\"73\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">15</text>\n\n    <path d=\"M 110,30 L 140,60\" stroke=\"#71717a\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"150\" cy=\"70\" r=\"14\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#3b82f6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"150\" y=\"73\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">30</text>\n\n    {/* Swap arrow */}\n    <path d=\"M 90,25 Q 70,40 60,55\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-heapify-down)\"/>\n    <text x=\"65\" y=\"35\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"monospace\">Swap</text>\n  </g>\n\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"arrow-heapify-down\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"5\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"4\" markerHeight=\"4\" orient=\"auto\">\n      <path d=\"M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\" fill=\"#ef4444\" />\n    </marker>\n  </defs>\n</svg>\n\n---\n\n## 3. Practice Checklist\n\n### Beginner Problems\n* [Last Stone Weight (LeetCode 1046)](https://leetcode.com/problems/last-stone-weight/): Basic heap operations.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Build heap insertion and extraction foundations.\n* [Kth Largest Element in a Stream (LeetCode 703)](https://leetcode.com/problems/kth-largest-element-in-a-stream/): Dynamic window sorting.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Teaches you to maintain running statistics.\n\n### Intermediate Problems\n* [Kth Largest Element in an Array (LeetCode 215)](https://leetcode.com/problems/kth-largest-element-in-an-array/): Heap-sort boundaries.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Optimizes search space limits using min-heaps.\n* [Merge k Sorted Lists (LeetCode 23)](https://leetcode.com/problems/merge-k-sorted-lists/): Multi-way merge bounds.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Leverages heaps to track smallest values across multiple streams.\n",
