    "markdown": "# Breadth-First Search (BFS): Level-Order Traversal, Shortest Path & Queue Mechanics\n\n**Breadth-First Search (BFS)** is a fundamental graph traversal algorithm that explores nodes layer by layer in expanding concentric circles around a starting source vertex. By using a First-In, First-Out (FIFO) queue, BFS guarantees the discovery of the **unweighted shortest path** between a source node and any reachable target node in a graph.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Level-Order Traversal & Algorithmic Mechanics\n\nBFS processes graph nodes in strict distance order: distance $0$ (the source vertex), distance $1$ (direct neighbors), distance $2$ (neighbors of neighbors), and so forth.\n\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 900 380\" width=\"100%\" height=\"auto\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <linearGradient id=\"bfsBg\" x1=\"0%\" y1=\"0%\" x2=\"100%\" y2=\"100%\">\n      <stop offset=\"0%\" stop-color=\"#0284c7\"/>\n      <stop offset=\"100%\" stop-color=\"#0f172a\"/>\n    </linearGradient>\n  </defs>\n  <rect width=\"900\" height=\"380\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"url(#bfsBg)\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"450\" y=\"40\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#f0f6fc\" font-family=\"system-ui, sans-serif\" font-size=\"20\" font-weight=\"700\">Breadth-First Search (BFS) Concentric Level Expansion</text>\n\n  <!-- Level 0 (Source) -->\n  <g transform=\"translate(180, 190)\">\n    <circle r=\"30\" fill=\"#0284c7\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n    <text x=\"0\" y=\"5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffffff\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-weight=\"700\" font-size=\"15\">S (L0)</text>\n  </g>\n\n  <!-- Level 1 Nodes -->\n  <g transform=\"translate(420, 110)\">\n    <circle r=\"26\" fill=\"#0369a1\" stroke=\"#7dd3fc\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <text x=\"0\" y=\"5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffffff\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-weight=\"700\" font-size=\"13\">A (L1)</text>\n  </g>\n  <g transform=\"translate(420, 270)\">\n    <circle r=\"26\" fill=\"#0369a1\" stroke=\"#7dd3fc\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <text x=\"0\" y=\"5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffffff\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-weight=\"700\" font-size=\"13\">B (L1)</text>\n  </g>\n  <path d=\"M 210 175 L 394 120\" stroke=\"#7dd3fc\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path d=\"M 210 205 L 394 260\" stroke=\"#7dd3fc\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n\n  <!-- Level 2 Nodes -->\n  <g transform=\"translate(680, 70)\">\n    <circle r=\"24\" fill=\"#075985\" stroke=\"#bae6fd\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"0\" y=\"4\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffffff\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"12\">C (L2)</text>\n  </g>\n  <g transform=\"translate(680, 190)\">\n    <circle r=\"24\" fill=\"#075985\" stroke=\"#bae6fd\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"0\" y=\"4\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffffff\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"12\">D (L2)</text>\n  </g>\n  <g transform=\"translate(680, 310)\">\n    <circle r=\"24\" fill=\"#075985\" stroke=\"#bae6fd\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"0\" y=\"4\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffffff\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"12\">E (L2)</text>\n  </g>\n  <path d=\"M 446 100 L 656 75\" stroke=\"#bae6fd\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <path d=\"M 446 120 L 656 180\" stroke=\"#bae6fd\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <path d=\"M 446 280 L 656 305\" stroke=\"#bae6fd\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n</svg>\n\n### Time and Space Complexities\n- **Time Complexity:** $O(V + E)$ where $V$ is the number of vertices and $E$ is the number of edges.\n- **Space Complexity:** $O(V)$ to maintain the FIFO queue and visited tracking set.\n\n---\n\n## 2. High-Performance C++ Graph BFS Implementation\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n#include <unordered_set>\n\nvoid bfsShortestPath(int startNode, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adjList) {\n    int n = adjList.size();\n    std::vector<int> dist(n, -1);\n    std::queue<int> q;\n\n    q.push(startNode);\n    dist[startNode] = 0;\n\n    while (!q.empty()) {\n        int u = q.front();\n        q.pop();\n\n        std::cout << \"Visited Node: \" << u << \" at Distance: \" << dist[u] << \"\\n\";\n\n        for (int v : adjList[u]) {\n            if (dist[v] == -1) {\n                dist[v] = dist[u] + 1;\n                q.push(v);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nint main() {\n    int V = 5;\n    std::vector<std::vector<int>> adj(V);\n    adj[0] = {1, 2};\n    adj[1] = {0, 3};\n    adj[2] = {0, 4};\n    adj[3] = {1};\n    adj[4] = {2};\n\n    std::cout << \"Starting BFS from node 0:\\n\";\n    bfsShortestPath(0, adj);\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs)\n\n### Q1: Why does BFS guarantee the shortest path in unweighted graphs, but fail in weighted graphs?\nBFS visits nodes in strictly non-decreasing order of edge hop distance. In an unweighted graph, edge hop distance equals path length. In weighted graphs, a path with 2 heavy edges can cost more than a path with 5 light edges; Dijkstra's algorithm (or Priority Queue BFS) must be used instead.\n",
