  "slug": "game-math-affine-space",
    "markdown": "# Game Mathematics: Affine Space (Points vs. Vectors)\n\nIn spatial computing and game engineering, maintaining a clear distinction between **Points** and **Vectors** is essential for correct geometric logic. While both can be represented as a set of coordinates, they have completely different mathematical properties.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Defining Points and Vectors\n\n*   A **Point** represents a specific position in space. It is absolute and anchored to the coordinate system. Examples include the position of a player, the location of a spawn point, or a vertex on a 3D model.\n*   A **Vector** represents a relative displacement, velocity, or direction. It has no fixed position. It describes a change in position or an offset.\n\n```\nPoint:   A fixed pin on a map.\nVector:  An arrow describing how to move.\n```\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 180\" width=\"100%\" height=\"180\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Point A -->\n  <circle cx=\"100\" cy=\"120\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#ef4444\" />\n  <text x=\"90\" y=\"145\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"13\">Point A (2, 4)</text>\n  \n  <!-- Point B -->\n  <circle cx=\"380\" cy=\"60\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#ef4444\" />\n  <text x=\"370\" y=\"85\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"13\">Point B (9, 7)</text>\n  \n  <!-- Vector v pointing from A to B -->\n  <line x1=\"100\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"380\" y2=\"60\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-blue)\" />\n  <text x=\"210\" y=\"80\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"14\">Vector v = B - A (7, 3)</text>\n  <text x=\"210\" y=\"100\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\">(Relative Displacement)</text>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\n### The Algebra of Affine Space\nBecause of their structural differences, operations between points and vectors are governed by specific rules:\n\n$$\\text{Point} - \\text{Point} = \\text{Vector}$$\n$$\\text{Point} + \\text{Vector} = \\text{Point} \\quad (\\text{translation of position})$$\n$$\\text{Vector} + \\text{Vector} = \\text{Vector} \\quad (\\text{combined displacements})$$\n$$\\text{Point} + \\text{Point} = \\text{Undefined} \\quad (\\text{meaningless operation})$$\n\n---\n\n## 2. Homogeneous Coordinates ($w$)\n\nTo handle both points and vectors uniformly in rendering and physics pipelines, we use a 4D coordinate system called **homogeneous coordinates**. We add a fourth component, $w$, to our 3D coordinates:\n\n$$\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{bmatrix} x \\\\ y \\\\ z \\\\ w \\end{bmatrix}$$\n\nThe value of $w$ determines the geometric nature of the entity:\n*   For a **Point**, we set $w = 1$.\n*   For a **Vector**, we set $w = 0$.\n\n### Why this distinction is critical\nWhen we perform spatial transformations, a translation matrix should shift the position of a Point, but it should leave a direction Vector unchanged (since translating a direction arrow doesn't change where it points). Homogeneous coordinates allow a single matrix transformation to automatically handle this difference.\n\n---\n\n## 3. The 4D Translation Matrix\n\nThe 4D translation matrix is defined as:\n\n$$T = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 & t_x \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & t_y \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & t_z \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}$$\n\n### 3.1 Applying Translation to a Point ($w = 1$)\nMultiplying $T$ by a point vector yields:\n\n$$\\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 & t_x \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & t_y \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & t_z \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\end{bmatrix} \\begin{bmatrix} x \\\\ y \\\\ z \\\\ 1 \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} x + t_x \\\\ y + t_y \\\\ z + t_z \\\\ 1 \\end{bmatrix}$$\n\nThe point is shifted correctly in space.\n\n### 3.2 Applying Translation to a Vector ($w = 0$)\nMultiplying $T$ by a direction vector yields:\n\n$$\\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 & t_x \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & t_y \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & t_z \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\end{bmatrix} \\begin{bmatrix} x \\\\ y \\\\ z \\\\ 0 \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} x \\\\ y \\\\ z \\\\ 0 \\end{bmatrix}$$\n\nBecause the $w$ component is $0$, the translation columns are multiplied by $0$, leaving the vector components completely unaffected by the translation. This allows game engines to process all geometry through a single, highly-optimized matrix pipeline.\n\n---\n\n## 4. The Perspective Divide\n\nSome operations, such as perspective projection matrices, modify the $w$ component of a coordinate, yielding a vector where $w \\neq 1$. To convert this back to standard 3D coordinate space, we perform a **perspective divide** (dividing all components by $w$):\n\n$$\\begin{bmatrix} x \\\\ y \\\\ z \\\\ w \\end{bmatrix} \\quad \\xrightarrow{\\text{divide by } w} \\quad \\begin{bmatrix} x/w \\\\ y/w \\\\ z/w \\\\ 1 \\end{bmatrix}$$\n\nIf $w = 0$, you cannot divide by it, reflecting the fact that a direction vector cannot be converted to a single point in space.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Implementation Example (Python)\n\n```python\nclass HomogeneousVector:\n    def __init__(self, x, y, z, w):\n        self.x = float(x)\n        self.y = float(y)\n        self.z = float(z)\n        self.w = float(w) # 1.0 for point, 0.0 for vector\n        \n    def is_point(self):\n        return self.w != 0.0\n        \n    def is_vector(self):\n        return self.w == 0.0\n        \n    def project(self):\n        if self.w == 0.0:\n            raise ValueError(\"Cannot perform perspective divide on a pure direction vector (w=0)\")\n        return (self.x / self.w, self.y / self.w, self.z / self.w)\n        \n    def __repr__(self):\n        type_str = \"Point\" if self.is_point() else \"Vector\"\n        return f\"{type_str}: [{self.x}, {self.y}, {self.z}, {self.w}]\"\n\ndef apply_translation(v, tx, ty, tz):\n    # Homogeneous translation simulation\n    new_x = v.x + tx * v.w\n    new_y = v.y + ty * v.w\n    new_z = v.z + tz * v.w\n    return HomogeneousVector(new_x, new_y, new_z, v.w)\n```\n\n\n---\n\n## 8. Conceptual Check questions\n\n**1. What is an Affine Space, and how does it differ from a standard vector space?**\n\n**Answer:**\nAn affine space is a set of points associated with a vector space. Unlike vector spaces, it has no distinguished origin point and does not support point addition.\n\n**2. Why is the distinction between a Point and a Vector critical in game physics?**\n\n**Answer:**\nA point represents a location in space, while a vector represents a direction or displacement. Translating a point shifts its position; translating a vector does nothing.\n\n**3. What are the rules for adding and subtracting points and vectors in an affine space?**\n\n**Answer:**\nPoint - Point = Vector; Point + Vector = Point; Vector + Vector = Vector. Point + Point is undefined.\n\n**4. Why is the addition of two points undefined in affine space?**\n\n**Answer:**\nBecause points represent absolute locations. Adding coordinates (like adding two street addresses) yields an arbitrary coordinate that depends entirely on where the coordinate system origin is placed.\n\n**5. How do barycentric combinations of points construct lines, triangles, and planes?**\n\n**Answer:**\nA weighted sum `P = sum(w_i * P_i)` represents a valid point if the weights sum to 1. Changing the weights defines coordinates along lines or across surface planes.\n\n**6. How does the homogeneous coordinate w distinguish a point (w = 1) from a vector (w = 0)?**\n\n**Answer:**\nWhen multiplying by a 4x4 matrix, a point `(x, y, z, 1)` is affected by the translation column. A vector `(x, y, z, 0)` ignores translation, preserving only its direction.\n\n**7. In what scenarios does neglecting affine space rules lead to physics errors (like incorrect translation offsets)?**\n\n**Answer:**\nWhen normal vectors are transformed using translation matrices, causing them to skew, scale, and point in incorrect directions.",
    "title": "Affine Space",
