  "slug": "game-math-spline",
    "markdown": "# Game Mathematics: Curves & Splines\n\nIn game development, objects rarely move only in straight lines. Whether it is a camera smoothly tracking a player, a racing car traversing a track, or an animated character moving their arm, we need mathematical tools to represent smooth, curved paths. To achieve this, we use **parametric curves** and **splines**.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Parametric Curves\n\nA standard function $y = f(x)$ has a major limitation: it cannot represent vertical lines or loops because each input $x$ can only map to a single output $y$. \n\nTo represent arbitrary paths in 2D or 3D space, we use **parametric equations**. We define each coordinate axis as a separate function of an independent parameter $t$ (usually representing time, ranging from $0$ to $1$):\n\n$$\\mathbf{P}(t) = \\begin{pmatrix} x(t) \\\\ y(t) \\\\ z(t) \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad t \\in [0, 1]$$\n\nBy changing the parameter $t$, we trace out a curve in space.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Defining a Spline\n\nA single mathematical formula (like a high-degree polynomial) becomes highly unstable and difficult to control when stretched over a long, complex path. This instability is known as *Runge's phenomenon*, where the curve starts to oscillate wildly at the ends.\n\nTo solve this, we construct a **spline**. A spline is a piecewise curve composed of multiple lower-degree polynomial segments (often cubic) joined together end-to-end. The join points are called **knots**.\n\n```\nSegment 1: P_1(t)       Segment 2: P_2(t)\n   \u25cf \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500> \u25cf \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500> \u25cf\n Knots:              Knot                Knot\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. Continuity Conditions\n\nTo ensure that the transitions between spline segments are visually smooth, we enforce mathematical **continuity conditions** at the knots.\n\n### 3.1 Geometric Continuity ($G^n$)\nGeometric continuity focuses on the visual smoothness of the path, ignoring the speed at which it is traversed:\n*   **$G^0$ Continuity:** The segments are connected. The end point of segment 1 is the start point of segment 2.\n*   **$G^1$ Continuity:** The tangent vectors at the knot point in the same direction, meaning there are no sharp corners.\n*   **$G^2$ Continuity:** The curvature is continuous.\n\n---\n\n### 3.2 Parametric Continuity ($C^n$)\nParametric continuity considers both the path shape and the speed of traversal. This is critical for physical camera paths or physics simulations:\n*   **$C^0$ Continuity (Position):** The segments are connected.\n    $$\\mathbf{P}_1(1) = \\mathbf{P}_2(0)$$\n*   **$C^1$ Continuity (Velocity):** The first derivatives (velocities) are equal at the join point. The movement does not instantly jerk or change speed.\n    $$\\mathbf{P}'_1(1) = \\mathbf{P}'_2(0)$$\n*   **$C^2$ Continuity (Acceleration):** The second derivatives (accelerations) are equal at the join point. The rate of speed change is smooth.\n    $$\\mathbf{P}''_1(1) = \\mathbf{P}''_2(0)$$\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 180\" width=\"100%\" height=\"180\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- C0: Sharp Join -->\n  <g transform=\"translate(0, 0)\">\n    <path d=\"M 30 130 Q 75 40 100 100\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#f43f5e\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n    <path d=\"M 100 100 Q 125 150 160 50\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#f43f5e\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n    <circle cx=\"100\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#f43f5e\" />\n    <text x=\"55\" y=\"160\" fill=\"#f43f5e\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\">C0 (Sharp corner)</text>\n  </g>\n  \n  <!-- C1: Smooth Join (Tangent aligned) -->\n  <g transform=\"translate(280, 0)\">\n    <path d=\"M 30 130 Q 80 50 100 100\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n    <path d=\"M 100 100 Q 120 150 170 80\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n    <circle cx=\"100\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" />\n    <!-- Tangent line guide -->\n    <line x1=\"70\" y1=\"75\" x2=\"130\" y2=\"125\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 2\" />\n    <text x=\"55\" y=\"160\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\">C1 (Smooth tangent)</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\n---\n\n## 4. Cubic Splines\n\nIn game math, **cubic polynomials** (degree 3) are the industry standard for spline segments:\n\n$$\\mathbf{P}(t) = \\mathbf{a} t^3 + \\mathbf{b} t^2 + \\mathbf{c} t + \\mathbf{d}$$\n\n### Why Cubic?\n*   **Lower-degree (Linear/Quadratic)** curves cannot achieve $C^2$ continuity or model complex inflection points (like an \"S\" curve) in a single segment.\n*   **Higher-degree (Quartic/Quintic)** polynomials require more computations and are prone to unwanted oscillations.\n*   Cubic polynomials provide the optimal balance between computational efficiency and smooth curvature control.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Implementation Example (Python)\n\nHere is a Python class evaluating a simple 1D piecewise linear spline to illustrate knot-matching intervals:\n\n```python\nclass PiecewiseLinearSpline:\n    def __init__(self, knots, values):\n        # knots: list of t parameters (e.g. [0.0, 0.5, 1.0])\n        # values: list of values at each knot (e.g. [0.0, 10.0, 2.0])\n        self.knots = knots\n        self.values = values\n        \n    def evaluate(self, t):\n        if t <= self.knots[0]:\n            return self.values[0]\n        if t >= self.knots[-1]:\n            return self.values[-1]\n            \n        # Find which interval t falls into\n        for i in range(len(self.knots) - 1):\n            if self.knots[i] <= t <= self.knots[i+1]:\n                # Interpolate locally in interval\n                t_local = (t - self.knots[i]) / (self.knots[i+1] - self.knots[i])\n                val_start = self.values[i]\n                val_end = self.values[i+1]\n                return val_start + t_local * (val_end - val_start)\n```\n\n\n---\n\n## 8. Conceptual Check questions\n\n**1. What is a mathematical spline, and how is it used in game development?**\n\n**Answer:**\nA spline is a piecewise polynomial curve. It is used to design camera paths, racing tracks, animation curves, and smooth AI pathing.\n\n**2. What is the difference between an interpolating spline and an approximating spline?**\n\n**Answer:**\nAn interpolating spline passes directly through all of its control points. An approximating spline uses control points as directional guides without passing through them.\n\n**3. What are control points, and how do they influence the shape of a spline curve?**\n\n**Answer:**\nThey are coordinate vertices in space. Moving a control point pulls the curve towards it, defining the local shape of the spline.\n\n**4. How is the parameter t (ranging from 0 to 1) used to traverse a spline path?**\n\n**Answer:**\n`t` acts as an interpolation variable. Evaluating the spline equation at `t` returns the coordinate position along that curve segment.\n\n**5. What does the continuity classification (C0, C1, C2) mean for the smoothness of a spline?**\n\n**Answer:**\n`C0` means segments are connected. `C1` means tangents match (velocity is continuous). `C2` means curvature matches (acceleration is continuous).\n\n**6. How do you calculate the velocity (first derivative) and acceleration (second derivative) at a point on a spline?**\n\n**Answer:**\nCompute the first derivative `P\\'(t)` to get the velocity vector, and the second derivative `P\\'\\'(t)` to get the acceleration vector.\n\n**7. How do you implement arc-length parametrization to ensure a camera moves at constant speed along a spline?**\n\n**Answer:**\nBy creating a lookup table mapping parameter `t` to physical distance traveled along the curve, allowing speed adjustments.",
