  "slug": "game-lang-gdscript",
    "markdown": "# Game Languages: GDScript in Game Development\n\n**GDScript** is a high-level, dynamically typed programming language built specifically for the **Godot** engine. Its syntax is heavily inspired by Python, but it is customized to integrate tightly with Godot's node-based architecture and component classes.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Key Features of GDScript\n\nGDScript is optimized for game development through several features:\n\n*   **First-Class Vector Math:** Primitives like `Vector2`, `Vector3`, `Transform3D`, and `Color` are built directly into the language syntax and VM.\n*   **Engine Integration:** Accessing nodes, handling signals, and loading resources are core operations of the language (e.g. using the `@onready` keyword).\n*   **Reference Counting:** Classes that inherit from `RefCounted` manage their own memory automatically, while Node components are managed explicitly by the scene tree.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Script-to-Engine Bindings\n\nWhen you write a GDScript, you are extending a native C++ class (such as `CharacterBody2D`). The Godot script engine maps calls between the GDScript interpreter and the compiled C++ core engine.\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 180\" width=\"100%\" height=\"180\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- GDScript box -->\n  <g transform=\"translate(40, 40)\">\n    <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" rx=\"4\" />\n    <text x=\"15\" y=\"30\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"12\">GDScript File</text>\n    <text x=\"15\" y=\"55\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">extends Node3D</text>\n    <text x=\"15\" y=\"75\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">_process(delta)</text>\n  </g>\n  \n  <!-- ClassDB Binder -->\n  <g transform=\"translate(210, 50)\">\n    <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"80\" height=\"70\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" fill-opacity=\"0.15\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" rx=\"4\" />\n    <text x=\"12\" y=\"38\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"12\">ClassDB</text>\n    <text x=\"10\" y=\"55\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"9\">Engine API</text>\n  </g>\n  \n  <!-- Native Engine class -->\n  <g transform=\"translate(320, 40)\">\n    <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\" fill=\"#10b981\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" rx=\"4\" />\n    <text x=\"15\" y=\"30\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"12\">C++ Core Class</text>\n    <text x=\"15\" y=\"55\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">CharacterBody3D</text>\n    <text x=\"15\" y=\"75\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">move_and_slide()</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\nThe **ClassDB** registry handles the translation layer, ensuring that variables and functions accessed in GDScript call the corresponding compiled C++ APIs instantly.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Static Typing in GDScript 2.0\n\nWith Godot 4.x, GDScript introduced major updates to its script compiler, including optional **static typing**.\n\nBy adding type hints to variables and functions:\n\n```gdscript\n# Dynamic Typing (Slow lookup)\nvar position = Vector2(0, 0)\n\n# Static Typing (Optimized lookup)\nvar position: Vector2 = Vector2(0, 0)\n```\n\nStatic typing allows the GDScript compiler to bypass runtime type-checking and generate direct bytecode offsets for access, resulting in significant execution speedups.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Implementation Example (GDScript)\n\nHere is a typical player movement script in GDScript, illustrating the built-in vector math and node references:\n\n```gdscript\nextends CharacterBody3D\n\n# Static configuration constants\nconst SPEED = 5.0\nconst JUMP_VELOCITY = 4.5\n\n# Reference to gravity from project settings\nvar gravity: float = ProjectSettings.get_setting(\"physics/3d/default_gravity\")\n\nfunc _physics_process(delta: float) -> void:\n    # 1. Apply gravity velocity\n    if not is_on_floor():\n        velocity.y -= gravity * delta\n\n    # 2. Get input vector and handle horizontal movement\n    var input_dir: Vector2 = Input.get_vector(\"ui_left\", \"ui_right\", \"ui_up\", \"ui_down\")\n    var direction: Vector3 = (transform.basis * Vector3(input_dir.x, 0, input_dir.y)).normalized()\n    \n    if direction != Vector3.ZERO:\n        velocity.x = direction.x * SPEED\n        velocity.z = direction.z * SPEED\n    else:\n        velocity.x = move_toward(velocity.x, 0, SPEED)\n        velocity.z = move_toward(velocity.z, 0, SPEED)\n\n    # 3. Call native move solver\n    move_and_slide()\n```\n\n\n---\n\n## 5. Conceptual Check questions\n\n**1. What is GDScript and why did Godot create a custom language instead of using Python?**\n\n**Answer:**\nGDScript is a dynamically-typed, Python-inspired scripting language built into Godot Engine. Godot chose a custom language over Python for: seamless engine integration (nodes, signals, and scenes are first-class), simpler embedding (no CPython runtime or GIL), native coroutine support via \\",
