  "slug": "game-phys-acceleration",
    "markdown": "# Game Physics: Linear & Angular Acceleration\n\nAcceleration measures the rate at which an object's velocity changes over time. In a physics engine, acceleration acts as the bridge connecting physical forces to updates in an object's position and orientation.\n\nWe divide acceleration into two fields: **Linear Acceleration** and **Angular Acceleration**.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Linear Acceleration\n\nLinear acceleration ($\\mathbf{a}$) is the rate of change of linear velocity ($\\mathbf{v}$). According to Newton's Second Law of Motion, acceleration is directly proportional to the net force ($\\mathbf{F}$) acting on an object and inversely proportional to its mass ($m$):\n\n$$\\mathbf{a} = \\frac{d\\mathbf{v}}{dt} = \\frac{\\mathbf{F}}{m}$$\n\nTo update a rigid body's velocity in the game loop, we integrate the acceleration over the frame's delta time:\n\n$$\\mathbf{v}_{\\text{new}} = \\mathbf{v}_{\\text{old}} + \\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\Delta t$$\n\n---\n\n## 2. Angular Acceleration & Torque\n\nAngular acceleration ($\\mathbf{\\alpha}$) is the rate of change of angular velocity ($\\mathbf{\\omega}$). It is generated by **Torque ($\\mathbf{\\tau}$)**, which is rotational force.\n\n### 2.1 Torque Calculation\nTorque is calculated as the cross product of the lever arm vector ($\\mathbf{r}$, pointing from the center of mass to where the force is applied) and the force vector ($\\mathbf{F}$):\n\n$$\\mathbf{\\tau} = \\mathbf{r} \\times \\mathbf{F}$$\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 220\" width=\"100%\" height=\"220\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Rotatable Body (Lever) -->\n  <line x1=\"120\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"380\" y2=\"140\" stroke=\"#27272a\" stroke-width=\"6\" />\n  \n  <!-- Center of mass (Axis) -->\n  <circle cx=\"120\" cy=\"140\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#ef4444\" />\n  <text x=\"90\" y=\"165\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\">Center of Mass</text>\n  \n  <!-- Lever Arm Vector r -->\n  <line x1=\"120\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"330\" y2=\"140\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-blue)\" />\n  <text x=\"210\" y=\"130\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\">r (Lever arm)</text>\n  \n  <!-- Force Vector F (Perpendicular) -->\n  <line x1=\"330\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"330\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-amber)\" />\n  <text x=\"340\" y=\"70\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\">F (Force)</text>\n  \n  <!-- Resulting Torque (Out of page, pointing up representation) -->\n  <text x=\"145\" y=\"185\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"14\">Torque \u03c4 = r \u00d7 F</text>\n  <path d=\"M 120 100 A 30 30 0 0 1 150 130\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-emerald)\" />\n</svg>\n</div>\n\n### 2.2 Calculating Angular Acceleration\nOnce we have the net torque, we calculate the angular acceleration ($\\mathbf{\\alpha}$) using the inverse of the **Inertia Tensor ($I$)**:\n\n$$\\mathbf{\\alpha} = \\frac{d\\mathbf{\\omega}}{dt} = I^{-1} \\mathbf{\\tau}$$\n\nUnlike linear mass, the inertia tensor can change orientation as the object rotates, meaning $I^{-1}$ must be updated and transformed in every frame.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Non-Inertial Reference Frames\n\nWhen calculations are performed inside a rotating frame of reference (like a player walking inside a spinning space station), we must apply **pseudo-forces** to simulate the correct physics:\n\n*   **Centrifugal Acceleration:** Points outward from the axis of rotation:\n    $$\\mathbf{a}_c = -\\mathbf{\\omega} \\times (\\mathbf{\\omega} \\times \\mathbf{r})$$\n*   **Coriolis Acceleration:** Appears when an object moves relative to the rotating frame, acting perpendicular to the direction of motion:\n    $$\\mathbf{a}_{\\text{cor}} = -2 (\\mathbf{\\omega} \\times \\mathbf{v})$$\n\n---\n\n## 4. Implementation Example (Python)\n\n```python\nclass RigidBodyPhysics:\n    def __init__(self, mass, inertia_tensor_inv):\n        self.mass = float(mass)\n        self.inv_inertia = inertia_tensor_inv # 3x3 matrix list\n        \n        self.linear_velocity = [0.0, 0.0, 0.0]\n        self.angular_velocity = [0.0, 0.0, 0.0]\n        \n    def apply_force(self, force, contact_point_offset, dt):\n        # 1. Update Linear Velocity: a = F / m\n        accel_x = force[0] / self.mass\n        accel_y = force[1] / self.mass\n        accel_z = force[2] / self.mass\n        \n        self.linear_velocity[0] += accel_x * dt\n        self.linear_velocity[1] += accel_y * dt\n        self.linear_velocity[2] += accel_z * dt\n        \n        # 2. Update Angular Velocity: torque = r x F\n        rx, ry, rz = contact_point_offset\n        fx, fy, fz = force\n        \n        torque_x = ry * fz - rz * fy\n        torque_y = rz * fx - rx * fz\n        torque_z = rx * fy - ry * fx\n        \n        # Multiply torque by inverse inertia tensor (alpha = I^-1 * torque)\n        alpha_x = self.inv_inertia[0][0]*torque_x + self.inv_inertia[0][1]*torque_y + self.inv_inertia[0][2]*torque_z\n        alpha_y = self.inv_inertia[1][0]*torque_x + self.inv_inertia[1][1]*torque_y + self.inv_inertia[1][2]*torque_z\n        alpha_z = self.inv_inertia[2][0]*torque_x + self.inv_inertia[2][1]*torque_y + self.inv_inertia[2][2]*torque_z\n        \n        self.angular_velocity[0] += alpha_x * dt\n        self.angular_velocity[1] += alpha_y * dt\n        self.angular_velocity[2] += alpha_z * dt\n```\n\n\n---\n\n## 7. Conceptual Check questions\n\n**1. What is the difference between linear and angular acceleration in game physics?**\n\n**Answer:**\nLinear acceleration (a = F/m) changes a body's translational velocity \u2014 it moves faster/slower or changes direction. Angular acceleration (\u03b1 = \u03c4/I) changes a body's rotational velocity (angular velocity $\\omega$) \u2014 it spins faster/slower. Both operate simultaneously: a baseball bat hit creates linear acceleration (ball flies away) and angular acceleration (ball spins with backspin). Physics engines compute and integrate both each frame independently.\\n\\n\n\n**2. How do physics engines integrate acceleration to update position and orientation?**\n\n**Answer:**\nStandard semi-implicit Euler integration:<br />1) Compute net force F and torque \u03c4 from all constraints and user forces.<br />2) Linear: a = F/m, v += a\u00d7dt, x += v\u00d7dt.<br />3) Angular: \u03b1 = I\u207b\u00b9\u00d7\u03c4, $\\omega$ += \u03b1\u00d7dt, orientation += integrate($\\omega$, dt). More accurate: Verlet integration (stores previous position, avoids storing velocity separately) or RK4 (Runge-Kutta 4th order) for high-accuracy simulations like spacecraft or cloth.\\n\\n\n\n**3. What is the difference between additive forces and impulses in a game physics engine?**\n\n**Answer:**\nForces accumulate over time (F applies over a frame: \u0394v = F\u00d7dt/m). Impulses change velocity instantly (\u0394v = J/m, where J is impulse in N\u00b7s). Use forces for: gravity, engine thrust, wind, continuous springs. Use impulses for: collision response (instantaneous velocity change at impact), explosions, weapon recoil. Physics APIs: Unity's \\",
